low","html_url":"https://github.com/zach-overflow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/zach-overflow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-23T17:41:38Z","updated_at":"2026-10-23T17:41:38Z","body":"@kadubhumika looks like [openapi-generator `7.26.0`](https://github.com/OpenAPITools/openapi-generator/releases/tag/v7.26.0) was released, which added `httpx2` support. Feel free to take a shot at it. Two things I think you'll need to watch out for and account for in your implementation:\n1. The openapi-generator, as far as I'm aware, does not natively have a way of merging the identical pydantic model class definitions from the sync and async parts of the generated library, so you will likely need to add a minor post-processing step which ensures both the sync and the async code imports the same model definitions. \n2. Similarly, the openapi-generator might have some odd function and method renaming if you enable both sync and async pathways. If that's the case, you'll want to make sure that the sync methods/functions are not changed compared to the current python client offering. If the tool does change the function/method names, then you'll need to either adjust the openapi-generator-cli's templating options, or again do some post-processing on the output files.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,